{"st":"FL","name":"Florida","seats":[{"census":1840,"seats":1},{"census":1850,"seats":1},{"census":1860,"seats":1},{"census":1870,"seats":2},{"census":1880,"seats":2},{"census":1890,"seats":2},{"census":1900,"seats":3},{"census":1910,"seats":4},{"census":1920,"seats":4},{"census":1930,"seats":5},{"census":1940,"seats":6},{"census":1950,"seats":8},{"census":1960,"seats":12},{"census":1970,"seats":15},{"census":1980,"seats":19},{"census":1990,"seats":23},{"census":2000,"seats":25},{"census":2010,"seats":27},{"census":2020,"seats":28}],"events":[{"congress":28,"year":1845,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Florida became the 27th state on March 3, 1845. Congress gave it 1 seat in the House until the next apportionment.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Florida kept 1 seat in the House. It elected its one member statewide (at-large).","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Florida kept 1 seat in the House. It elected its one member statewide (at-large).","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 1 to 2 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 43rd Congress (1873–1875), Florida elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Florida kept 2 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Florida kept 2 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 2 to 3 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 3 to 4 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 63rd Congress (1913–1915), Florida elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 4 to 5 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 73rd through 74th Congresses (1933–1937), Florida elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 5 to 6 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 78th Congress (1943–1945), Florida elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 6 to 8 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 8 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 12 to 15 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 15 to 19 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 19 to 23 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 23 to 25 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":111,"year":2010,"kind":"other","title":"Fair Districts amendments","text":"Florida voters approved Amendment 6 for congressional districts and Amendment 5 for legislative districts, adding rules to the state constitution that bar drawing districts to favor or disfavor a party or an incumbent.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/florida/","https://ballotpedia.org/Florida_Amendment_6,_Congressional_District_Boundaries_(2010)"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 25 to 27 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":115,"year":2015,"kind":"court","title":"Court orders a new congressional map","text":"In League of Women Voters of Florida v. Detzner the Florida Supreme Court found that the 2012 congressional map violated the Fair Districts rules and approved a remedial map in December 2015. It was used from the 2016 election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/florida/"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Florida's House delegation grew from 27 to 28 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":119,"year":2025,"kind":"court","title":"Black Voters Matter v. Byrd","text":"The Florida Supreme Court upheld the 2022 congressional map in July 2025, ruling that the state constitution's non-diminishment rule could not require restoring the former North Florida district.","sources":["https://www.wlrn.org/government-politics/2025-07-17/florida-supreme-court-upholds-desantis-backed-congressional-redistricting-plan"]},{"congress":120,"year":2026,"kind":"mid-decade","title":"Mid-decade redistricting","text":"The legislature passed a new congressional map in an April 2026 special session, and the governor signed it on May 4. It redrew 21 of the 28 districts and left Districts 1 through 7 unchanged. Courts declined to block it before the election, and it was used in 2026 while a lawsuit continued.","sources":["https://wusf.org/text/politics-issues/2026-05-06/breakdown-changes-for-florida-new-congressional-map","https://www.wlrn.org/light/government-politics/2026-05-04/desantis-signs-new-congressional-district-map","https://wtop.com/national/2026/06/florida-court-allows-use-of-new-us-house-districts-drawn-by-republicans-for-midterm-elections/"]}],"compactness":{"c029":{"FL-0":0.039},"c030":{"FL-0":0.039},"c031":{"FL-0":0.039},"c032":{"FL-0":0.039},"c033":{"FL-0":0.039},"c034":{"FL-0":0.039},"c035":{"FL-0":0.039},"c036":{